WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.840
Hello again! Welcome to another episode of&nbsp;
community questions that I'm allowed to host!

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.480
I've decided to move the&nbsp;
format around a little bi:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.240
All these questions are in some way&nbsp;
about regularization techniques.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.880
So instead of going through them rather randomly&nbsp;
I'll try to explain things a bit more orderily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.840
Feel free to skip to either&nbsp;
part using the timestamps below.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.520
So, what is regularization?&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.840
Yes everyone who writes it with a "z"&nbsp;
is a bloody savage, sorry Letitia!

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.360
A regularization is any technique&nbsp;
that helps your neural network to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.520
solve your objective function in a better way.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.240
Put simplym your objective or loss&nbsp;
function tells your net what to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.520
and regularization is added to&nbsp;
tell it how it should do that.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.120
As a simple example consider fitting&nbsp;
a function through these given points.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.520
While the red curve does hit every&nbsp;
sample point it generalizes badly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.800
for the samples not seen during&nbsp;
training – it is overfitting.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.280
A regularization term here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.120
could enforce smoothness giving you&nbsp;
that nice green curve that you can see.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.920
Usually, these regularizations are added&nbsp;
as an extra term to the loss function.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.760
So, two very common regularizations that are&nbsp;
already built in in most optimizers such as Adam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.720
would be l1 and Tikhonov regularization,&nbsp;
that is also known as l2 or weight decay.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.600
L1 regularization just adds the absolute sum&nbsp;
of all weights of the network to the loss term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.080
whereas Tikhonov or l2 regularization&nbsp;
does the same for the squared weights.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.320
In practice that just means that L1 does&nbsp;
not care which weights we actually touch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.640
as long as we are always getting lower in total.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.040
For l2 regularization this changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.200
as we square each weight this is always&nbsp;
punishing the highest weight the most.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.680
So, while l2 regularization and Tikhonov mean the&nbsp;
same, l2 regularization shrinks the weights more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.800
evenly than l1 preferring to punish the higher&nbsp;
weights more than the lower ones . And l2 does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.040
this in a linear way always just preferring&nbsp;
to get the total sum of the weights down.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.160
If we reconsider our example from the beginning,&nbsp;
we can easily see why having either regularization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.280
term is helpful: To form a curve such as the&nbsp;
red line we'd need much higher weights. Very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.320
informally spoken – please don't hit me for that&nbsp;
– sharper turns of the output function require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.600
larger weights, whereas for example a flat line&nbsp;
can be produced just with all weights being zero.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.320
So, this also helps against&nbsp;
overfitting while improving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.120
generalization. By preferring&nbsp;
the green over the red line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.640
easy solutions just tend to generalize better than&nbsp;
memorizing every last bit of the training data.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.680
Now let's look at one regularization&nbsp;
for the analysis (calculus) lovers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.640
Let's quickly repeat what Lipschitz&nbsp;
continuity is and see how we can use that to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.400
for example better train GANs or&nbsp;
defend against adversarial attacks.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.440
A function is Lipschitz continuous with the&nbsp;
Lipschitz constant c, if a change applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.960
to its input value never changes the output&nbsp;
value by more than c times that input change.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.720
Or, with other words there is some upper&nbsp;
limit to the slope of this function.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.560
As our networks are also just&nbsp;
piecewise linear functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.120
we can also compute the&nbsp;
Lipschitz constant for them.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.360
Discretely spoken, this means that for&nbsp;
any two samples of the input their output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.840
distance relative to the input distance&nbsp;
should at most be that Lipschitz constant.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.800
Meaning, for a network that&nbsp;
is Lipschitz continuous when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.800
we fiddle around with one single input,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.840
the output change occurring has some upper bound.&nbsp;
And that is already the answer to this question.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.800
A simple way of enforcing Lipschitz&nbsp;
continuity is the so-called weight clipping.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.680
The output of a network always depends on its&nbsp;
weights and when we simply set an upper limit to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.480
the absolute value of a weight, that is we just&nbsp;
clip any weight that is larger than this limit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.360
we already obtain a very crude limit on how much&nbsp;
the change in the input can change the output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.040
leaving us with a Lipschitz bound.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.520
it's just the same argument as before&nbsp;
for the l1 and l2 regularization.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:47.280
High slope needs higher values as coefficient,&nbsp;
meaning high weights in the network.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.960
And by enforcing that our weights&nbsp;
are small, we make sure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.160
learned function does not have any of&nbsp;
these sharp turns that we do not want.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.080
Now, let's have a look at what else this is&nbsp;
good for: imagine that you want to train a GAN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.080
and I'm explicitly saying "imagine" here,&nbsp;
because these things are a pain to train.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.320
So you train your GAN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.320
and during training your discriminator&nbsp;
becomes way too good for the generator.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.240
That means that you suddenly have a strong&nbsp;
jump in your discriminator and meaning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.960
when your generator is producing super fake&nbsp;
outputs slightly changing something as you'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.080
usually do when training networks, won't help at&nbsp;
all: your output will still be very much fake.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.840
Hence you basically end up with&nbsp;
a vanishing gradient problem;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.840
there is just no clear direction in which&nbsp;
your gradient points that can help you.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.960
You'd need to change a whole lot&nbsp;
of things and then suddenly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.400
discriminator gets to this jump and&nbsp;
thinks your generated output is real.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.040
And this of course means that you can't&nbsp;
properly train your generator anymore.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.040
Big problem lots of headache,&nbsp;
so what do you do about that?

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.920
Well you don't want these jumps in&nbsp;
your loss function. You want your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.280
loss function to smoothly change&nbsp;
with a limited rate of change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.560
which is exactly what a Lipschitz&nbsp;
bound for your network does.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.400
So, for example you enforce Lipschitz&nbsp;
continuity through weight clipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.920
and boom! things get better. Mostly.&nbsp;
Sometimes. GANs are lots of fun!

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.480
Now, before you run off and use that knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.680
don't! Because there is an even&nbsp;
better regularization technique.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.440
Lipschitz continuity prevents you&nbsp;
from having steep changes in the loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.160
but it does not prevent local optima or the&nbsp;
function changing only slowly or not at all.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.080
Much better than that, we can simply say that&nbsp;
our network should not be Lipschitz bound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.960
but instead also exactly change by some&nbsp;
constant relative to the input change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.040
meaning our regularization term is&nbsp;
just enforcing a constant gradient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.400
for example one everywhere, which in theory&nbsp;
means that there is no local optimum anymore!

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.960
A function, a neural network, with a&nbsp;
constant gradient like one everywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.080
has not one spot where the gradient is zero! It&nbsp;
is just some linear function and as we all know a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:54.720
local optimum has always a gradient of zero and on&nbsp;
such a linear function we can train even better.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.880
Lastly, Lipschitz regularization can&nbsp;
also help us against adversarial attacks.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.600
For adversarial attacks we usually&nbsp;
look for a tiny shift in the input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.000
that causes a large shift in the output.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.960
For example, when we add almost invisible&nbsp;
noise perturbations to this image of a panda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:14.800
we can make it look like a given to&nbsp;
a classifier that we trained before.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.040
Well, if only there was such a thing&nbsp;
to make sure these tiny input changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.200
do not totally change the output!

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.680
Well, again Lipschitz regularization to the&nbsp;
rescue! Enforcing a somewhat low Lipschitz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.000
constant for the network, means that for&nbsp;
an input that is only slightly changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.040
we cannot change the output by much. Or in&nbsp;
other words, we need to shift a lot more pixels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.200
much more visibly to change this panda into&nbsp;
a gibbon in the eyes of our classifier.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.720
So, I hope that this was somewhat&nbsp;
useful to you. As always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.760
I would be very happy for some feedback&nbsp;
in the comments and we'll leave you some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.000
additional links and sources&nbsp;
in the comments (description).

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:48.560
Bye bye!

